Add a persistent status bar for the PiBoy DMG: clock left, status right.

A bar over both the frontend and running games is normally a compositor job -
under full KMS only the DRM master may add an overlay plane, and that is
EmulationStation or RetroArch, never a helper process. The legacy stack this
device shipped with drew one through dispmanx, which full KMS removes.

RetroArch already has the mechanism, though. status_text, built from fps_show,
framecount_show and memory_show, is pushed every frame with duration 1 and
flush=true - precisely a permanent overlay. This adds one more block to that
chain behind a status_bar_show setting: no compositor, no Wayland rebuild, no
change to input routing.

Layout follows the existing anchors. status_text is right-anchored off
top_right_x_advance and the paused/fast-forward/rewind indicators march
leftwards from it, so the clock travels separately in status_bar_clock and is
drawn at x=0 where nothing else can collide with it.

Fields: battery with charge state, SoC temperature, strongest wireless signal,
and volume. All are small sysfs/procfs reads and none changes faster than once
a minute, so they refresh on the FPS counter's cadence. Volume is the exception
- it must feel live - so it is polled every few frames, but only a stat() until
the mtime moves. Any field whose file is missing is omitted, so this degrades
field by field rather than failing.

The wireless level is chosen by signal strength rather than by interface name.
A hotplug renumbers wlanN, and on this machine the good adapter is not reliably
wlan0 - assuming otherwise once cost an evening.

Volume shows as a plain percentage rather than a drawn bar: the widget renderer
loads its own proportional font from the asset pack, not video_font_path, so a
bar of '=' and '-' would change width as it filled.

Note the gate is duplicated. video_driver.c decides whether to publish
status_text; gfx_widgets.c decides independently whether to draw it. Both list
the same flags and BOTH must know about the new one, or the string is built and
silently never rendered. The four simple_widget_height offsets are extended too,
so the indicators clear the status row instead of drawing over it.

This needs menu_enable_widgets=true if anything else uses SHOW_MSG: with widgets
off, status_text shares the notification queue and its per-frame flush=true
clears it; with widgets on it has its own slot and the two coexist.

The separator test is deliberately buf_pos > 0 rather than
status_text[buf_pos-1] != '\0' as the blocks above use. Theirs reads
status_text[-1] when nothing has been appended yet.

